00:00:46.041 --> 00:00:49.380
Thank you all of you for
watching my presentation.

00:00:49.381 --> 00:00:51.630
This is the outline
of my presentation.

00:00:51.631 --> 00:00:53.675
It starts with
a little introduction

00:00:53.676 --> 00:00:55.640
on Audio Quality Assessment.

00:00:55.641 --> 00:00:59.180
Then we dedicate some time
to explain our dataset

00:00:59.181 --> 00:01:02.560
which took a massive amount
of time to develop,

00:01:02.561 --> 00:01:04.710
and it's a really nice dataset.

00:01:04.711 --> 00:01:08.330
Then we discuss
our proposed approach

00:01:08.331 --> 00:01:10.844
for the plan of
Audio Quality Assessment,

00:01:10.845 --> 00:01:13.760
and then the results
in the conclusion.

00:01:13.761 --> 00:01:16.550
So, what is Audio
Quality Assessment?

00:01:16.551 --> 00:01:18.485
The idea of the Audio
Quality Assessment

00:01:18.486 --> 00:01:20.855
is to estimate the quality of

00:01:20.856 --> 00:01:23.150
the audio based not just on

00:01:23.151 --> 00:01:26.265
the signal attributes
such as SNR,

00:01:26.266 --> 00:01:29.995
noise level, speech
level or reverberation,

00:01:29.996 --> 00:01:33.340
but also considering
the human factor,

00:01:33.341 --> 00:01:34.950
the human subjectiveness.

00:01:34.951 --> 00:01:36.245
So, at the end,

00:01:36.246 --> 00:01:38.330
we want a model that can give us

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:42.425
a number that we will
represent the quality.

00:01:42.426 --> 00:01:44.380
Why this is important.

00:01:44.381 --> 00:01:48.540
So, many multimedia
services network systems,

00:01:48.541 --> 00:01:50.025
they need to be monitored,

00:01:50.026 --> 00:01:53.420
and we can not just look at
the quality of the services

00:01:53.421 --> 00:01:55.790
without considering
or taking into

00:01:55.791 --> 00:01:58.475
consideration the end user.

00:01:58.476 --> 00:02:00.530
What we could do was to have

00:02:00.531 --> 00:02:03.050
a feedback from the end
user to monitor ourselves,

00:02:03.051 --> 00:02:05.005
but this is not always happening.

00:02:05.006 --> 00:02:07.610
So, an alternative is to
have a model that can tell

00:02:07.611 --> 00:02:10.520
us how good is our service,

00:02:10.521 --> 00:02:14.560
also considering
the perspective of the user.

00:02:14.561 --> 00:02:17.560
So, at the end, we don't
want to predicting

00:02:17.561 --> 00:02:20.270
a voice call and
avoid the user having

00:02:20.271 --> 00:02:22.700
problems of not hearing

00:02:22.701 --> 00:02:25.210
each other or not

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:27.115
being able to synchronize
the conversation.

00:02:27.116 --> 00:02:29.270
So, all these things
can be monitored and

00:02:29.271 --> 00:02:32.610
improved if you have
a feedback from the user.

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:35.015
So, these are some of

00:02:35.016 --> 00:02:38.240
the possible existence of
network impairments that

00:02:38.241 --> 00:02:40.780
can hinder a call

00:02:40.781 --> 00:02:43.780
or can compromise the
quality of the audio.

00:02:43.781 --> 00:02:47.360
So, latency, jitter, packet loss,

00:02:47.361 --> 00:02:52.380
and on the signal side,

00:02:52.381 --> 00:02:54.435
you can have speech
distortion, such as,

00:02:54.436 --> 00:02:57.170
additive noise,
reverberation, clipping.

00:02:57.171 --> 00:02:59.300
So, all these kinds
of artifacts can

00:02:59.301 --> 00:03:03.450
hinder communication
based on audio.

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:07.960
So, how we can

00:03:07.961 --> 00:03:10.310
actually assess the quality
of the audio there.

00:03:10.311 --> 00:03:11.410
We have two alternatives.

00:03:11.411 --> 00:03:16.870
One of them, is to have people
assessing the audio files.

00:03:16.871 --> 00:03:24.810
So, we can have a
speech being recorded,

00:03:24.811 --> 00:03:26.605
then people can listen to it,

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:30.555
and then each participant
can give a score,

00:03:30.556 --> 00:03:33.600
then you get what's called MOS.

00:03:33.601 --> 00:03:35.080
So, MOS is just the mean or

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:36.895
the average of all the scores,

00:03:36.896 --> 00:03:39.279
and this is important
because it eliminates

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.030
the subjectiveness
of each individual.

00:03:42.031 --> 00:03:44.805
So, each person has their own
reference of quality.

00:03:44.806 --> 00:03:48.080
It depends on
many different aspects,

00:03:48.081 --> 00:03:49.920
for instance, the person
is an expert,

00:03:49.921 --> 00:03:51.125
they might be more rigid.

00:03:51.126 --> 00:03:54.610
If the call or the audio or
the content is important,

00:03:54.611 --> 00:03:56.100
it may also influence the score.

00:03:56.101 --> 00:03:57.650
So, with the MOS,

00:03:57.651 --> 00:03:59.285
we can eliminate these factors.

00:03:59.286 --> 00:04:02.360
So, subjective listening test

00:04:02.361 --> 00:04:04.345
is actually
the most reliable one,

00:04:04.346 --> 00:04:06.105
but it has some drawbacks.

00:04:06.106 --> 00:04:11.425
So, it's time-consuming, it's
laborious, it's expensive,

00:04:11.426 --> 00:04:14.600
and I think one of
the things that is

00:04:14.601 --> 00:04:17.390
really complicated is that

00:04:17.391 --> 00:04:19.225
it cannot be done in real-time.

00:04:19.226 --> 00:04:22.090
So, if we have a model
that can do this for you,

00:04:22.091 --> 00:04:26.790
you can actually evaluate
your systems on the fly.

00:04:26.791 --> 00:04:30.625
So, another alternative to

00:04:30.626 --> 00:04:32.830
subjective listening test is to

00:04:32.831 --> 00:04:36.245
compute MOS using an algorithm.

00:04:36.246 --> 00:04:40.265
So, we replace the human by
a computer based algorithm.

00:04:40.266 --> 00:04:44.200
We have two types of algorithms.

00:04:44.201 --> 00:04:45.550
Can be signal based,

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:48.480
so the algorithm looks at
the signal and extract some

00:04:48.481 --> 00:04:51.770
attribute operands of the signal
to estimate the quality.

00:04:51.771 --> 00:04:53.360
We have a parametric base,

00:04:53.361 --> 00:04:58.960
which use network parameters
to estimate the quality,

00:04:58.961 --> 00:05:00.985
and you can have a hybrid one.

00:05:00.986 --> 00:05:04.100
So, we can use

00:05:04.101 --> 00:05:10.555
the signal without reference
to estimate the quality,

00:05:10.556 --> 00:05:14.290
or we can use a double ended
one which use the reference.

00:05:14.291 --> 00:05:16.300
So, you have the degraded signal,

00:05:16.301 --> 00:05:19.605
the clean signal and then
you compare the two.

00:05:19.606 --> 00:05:22.960
That's what PESQ does,

00:05:22.961 --> 00:05:27.640
we are going to talk about
these metrics in a little bit.

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:31.435
So, these are some of

00:05:31.436 --> 00:05:35.834
the mostly known metrics

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:38.530
or measures for
Audio Quality Assessment.

00:05:38.531 --> 00:05:42.340
We have PESQ, a new version which

00:05:42.341 --> 00:05:46.990
can be used for
wide band signal, POLQA.

00:05:46.991 --> 00:05:52.755
These are all developed by
the ITU-T organization,

00:05:52.756 --> 00:05:54.585
and you can see
that, for instance,

00:05:54.586 --> 00:06:01.010
PESQ is more strained for
codecs and compressions,

00:06:01.011 --> 00:06:04.980
not really for noise
and reverberation,

00:06:04.981 --> 00:06:06.020
or the kind of environments

00:06:06.021 --> 00:06:07.475
that we can have in the signal.

00:06:07.476 --> 00:06:10.080
We actually have
some limitations,

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:13.385
therefore this is actually

00:06:13.386 --> 00:06:15.330
the motivation for our project.

00:06:15.331 --> 00:06:18.195
So, although PESQ is widely used,

00:06:18.196 --> 00:06:19.660
it has some drawbacks.

00:06:19.661 --> 00:06:22.965
It was not really
trained for distortions

00:06:22.966 --> 00:06:30.849
introduced by
the speech compression,

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:33.250
but it is intrusive.

00:06:33.251 --> 00:06:35.770
So, it needs the clean speech or

00:06:35.771 --> 00:06:39.545
a reference speech to
compute the output.

00:06:39.546 --> 00:06:43.460
So, our objective is to have

00:06:43.461 --> 00:06:45.260
a new algorithm that can

00:06:45.261 --> 00:06:46.965
be trained on
noise reverberation,

00:06:46.966 --> 00:06:49.290
audio pipeline distortions,

00:06:49.291 --> 00:06:51.805
and also that is not interesting.

00:06:51.806 --> 00:06:56.770
So, these are the two main
things that we're looking for.

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:01.095
When you look at
the literature review,

00:07:01.096 --> 00:07:02.650
where actually in this project,

00:07:02.651 --> 00:07:04.310
we're focusing more on

00:07:04.311 --> 00:07:07.540
deep learning approach
to build our model.

00:07:07.541 --> 00:07:09.380
When you look at
the literature review,

00:07:09.381 --> 00:07:12.620
we see that there
is a lot of work

00:07:12.621 --> 00:07:15.050
on using deep learning

00:07:15.051 --> 00:07:18.140
for predicting
mostly intelligibility,

00:07:18.141 --> 00:07:22.040
but not really for speech
quality in general.

00:07:22.041 --> 00:07:23.440
Most of these
approaches are based

00:07:23.441 --> 00:07:25.950
on a speech recognition,

00:07:25.951 --> 00:07:27.220
so they actually estimate

00:07:27.221 --> 00:07:31.000
the quality considering
the performance

00:07:31.001 --> 00:07:33.380
of the speech
recognition systems.

00:07:33.381 --> 00:07:37.790
There are two approaches
that I thought was.

00:07:37.791 --> 00:07:41.200
&gt;&gt; Most of the papers
are from this year.

00:07:41.201 --> 00:07:41.890
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:07:41.891 --> 00:07:47.275
&gt;&gt; Except one. So, it's
a new thinking in the community.

00:07:47.276 --> 00:07:50.305
&gt;&gt; Yes. Those are papers
that are recent papers.

00:07:50.306 --> 00:07:53.710
They're focused on deep learning
to solve this problem,

00:07:53.711 --> 00:07:57.070
but most of them focus
on intelligibility.

00:07:57.071 --> 00:08:00.410
Intelligibility is
actually just one factor

00:08:00.411 --> 00:08:03.030
or one aspect of quality.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:06.925
So, this paper I thought
was interesting,

00:08:06.926 --> 00:08:09.530
although it's also focused
on intelligibility.

00:08:09.531 --> 00:08:11.405
But, the idea here is to

00:08:11.406 --> 00:08:14.360
have a spectrogram of
your speech signal,

00:08:14.361 --> 00:08:18.185
and then use a CNN
and their kernels

00:08:18.186 --> 00:08:23.100
as a filter to estimate the MOS.

00:08:23.101 --> 00:08:25.295
It actually eliminates some of

00:08:25.296 --> 00:08:27.260
the engineering features that we

00:08:27.261 --> 00:08:32.770
know by using a CNN as
a filter and as a kernel.

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:37.475
This one was also a paper that
I thought was interesting.

00:08:37.476 --> 00:08:40.760
It's an end-to-end non-intrusive

00:08:40.761 --> 00:08:42.840
speech quality assessment model,

00:08:42.841 --> 00:08:45.770
and it uses a spectrogram

00:08:45.771 --> 00:08:48.770
and a LSTM to
estimate the quality.

00:08:48.771 --> 00:08:51.470
So, I thought it was
interesting that they built

00:08:51.471 --> 00:08:55.040
actually their own cost
function, they customized it,

00:08:55.041 --> 00:08:58.730
and to get the correlation

00:08:58.731 --> 00:09:02.350
between each samples or
the sequence of samples here,

00:09:02.351 --> 00:09:05.845
they estimate the
quality by frame.

00:09:05.846 --> 00:09:08.315
So, usually what we have is

00:09:08.316 --> 00:09:09.700
the overall quality for

00:09:09.701 --> 00:09:12.105
the whole utterance,
and in this paper,

00:09:12.106 --> 00:09:15.694
they actually were able to
export this information

00:09:15.695 --> 00:09:18.020
to estimate the quality

00:09:18.021 --> 00:09:21.440
of bi-frame and then get
the overall quality.

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:27.855
We'll now talk about
the data generation.

00:09:27.856 --> 00:09:31.450
So, we had to spend
quite a few time

00:09:31.451 --> 00:09:33.525
to try to generate

00:09:33.526 --> 00:09:36.765
more realistic dataset with
different impairments.

00:09:36.766 --> 00:09:42.000
We had initially a clean
speech, 2,000 files,

00:09:42.001 --> 00:09:46.130
then we convert it with
different impulse response,

00:09:46.131 --> 00:09:50.485
to simulate reverberation
distance talk.

00:09:50.486 --> 00:09:56.710
We had the reverberant speech
samples, then added noise,

00:09:56.711 --> 00:09:59.310
we had like four categories of

00:09:59.311 --> 00:10:03.465
noise and with different SNR,

00:10:03.466 --> 00:10:07.360
and also different speech
levels and noise levels.

00:10:07.361 --> 00:10:08.400
We're going to show

00:10:08.401 --> 00:10:11.900
all the statistics on the
data in a few slides.

00:10:11.901 --> 00:10:14.580
At the end, we had
like 10,000 noise

00:10:14.581 --> 00:10:21.590
samples and to train our model
we divide it this way.

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:27.480
So, I want to now talk a little

00:10:27.481 --> 00:10:31.620
bit about how we labeled
the data. Before I just-

00:10:31.621 --> 00:10:33.585
&gt;&gt; One question,
from previous slide.

00:10:33.586 --> 00:10:36.190
Upper on that corner,

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:40.965
third line the random
noise level of 50 dB SPL,

00:10:40.966 --> 00:10:45.250
deviation of zero degree
or it's a typo?

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:51.105
&gt;&gt; Yes, it's a typo.
It should be 10 dB.

00:10:51.106 --> 00:10:52.750
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:58.639
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, this
is just a model,

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:02.795
how the quantities
perceived by the humans.

00:11:02.796 --> 00:11:05.885
They actually are
divided in three events.

00:11:05.886 --> 00:11:07.350
The first event, is
the physical event,

00:11:07.351 --> 00:11:11.010
is the waveform
which are the ears.

00:11:11.011 --> 00:11:13.875
We perceive the sound then,

00:11:13.876 --> 00:11:15.705
we have the perceptual event,

00:11:15.706 --> 00:11:18.900
where we actually extracted
the features such

00:11:18.901 --> 00:11:23.475
as loud noise reverberation.

00:11:23.476 --> 00:11:27.270
So, we automatically extract

00:11:27.271 --> 00:11:28.810
those information from the audio

00:11:28.811 --> 00:11:30.510
and compare with
the desired features.

00:11:30.511 --> 00:11:32.570
So, the design features
is the internal reference

00:11:32.571 --> 00:11:34.925
that each person has,

00:11:34.926 --> 00:11:36.880
and then we can make a judgment.

00:11:36.881 --> 00:11:38.465
So, after doing the judgement,

00:11:38.466 --> 00:11:43.595
we were able to estimate
the quality ourselves,

00:11:43.596 --> 00:11:46.855
and then maybe have
a description of what effect

00:11:46.856 --> 00:11:51.140
was played a role on
this mission of quality.

00:11:51.141 --> 00:11:54.735
So, basically, this
is what will happen

00:11:54.736 --> 00:11:59.000
when we have people rating
our speech file, our samples.

00:11:59.001 --> 00:12:02.555
&gt;&gt; What is PJDD in the boxes?.

00:12:02.556 --> 00:12:05.535
&gt;&gt; This is the perception,

00:12:05.536 --> 00:12:08.575
to judgment, this
is the description.

00:12:08.576 --> 00:12:10.075
So, we have the perception of

00:12:10.076 --> 00:12:12.305
the physical event,
which are wave-forms.

00:12:12.306 --> 00:12:15.900
We measured the
features internally.

00:12:15.901 --> 00:12:18.550
So, that's the perceptual event.

00:12:18.551 --> 00:12:19.830
We compared them with

00:12:19.831 --> 00:12:22.745
the desired feature which
is your internal reference,

00:12:22.746 --> 00:12:25.950
then you can make a judgment
how good is the quality.

00:12:25.951 --> 00:12:27.260
So, the thing is that people

00:12:27.261 --> 00:12:29.540
have different desired features.

00:12:29.541 --> 00:12:32.715
So, we have different
expectations,

00:12:32.716 --> 00:12:35.354
then we'll do the description.

00:12:35.355 --> 00:12:38.255
So, the idea of the MOS is to

00:12:38.256 --> 00:12:42.360
minimize or mitigate this device.

00:12:42.361 --> 00:12:53.310
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:12:53.311 --> 00:12:54.590
&gt;&gt; So, he's like considering

00:12:54.591 --> 00:12:57.260
the desired features
with reference

00:12:57.261 --> 00:13:01.465
and here's just considering
the degraded signal.

00:13:01.466 --> 00:13:03.450
So, here it would be like the

00:13:03.451 --> 00:13:05.790
non-intrusive and here intrusive

00:13:05.791 --> 00:13:06.945
because you have reference.

00:13:06.946 --> 00:13:08.260
&gt;&gt; I see.

00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:14.490
&gt;&gt; So, I thought it was
important to before

00:13:14.491 --> 00:13:17.360
talking about
our listening tests or

00:13:17.361 --> 00:13:20.590
our listening quality tests to

00:13:20.591 --> 00:13:22.990
make clear what are

00:13:22.991 --> 00:13:25.645
the effects that people
are listening to.

00:13:25.646 --> 00:13:29.325
So, we have the
reverberant speech,

00:13:29.326 --> 00:13:33.090
with RT60 ranging from

00:13:33.091 --> 00:13:35.670
300 milliseconds to
500 milliseconds,

00:13:35.671 --> 00:13:37.635
in our discussion was

00:13:37.636 --> 00:13:42.100
the most common range

00:13:42.101 --> 00:13:45.605
and had a close talk
and in anechoic speech.

00:13:45.606 --> 00:13:47.640
So, in terms of SNR,

00:13:47.641 --> 00:13:50.240
this is basically
the range of our SNR,

00:13:50.241 --> 00:13:51.740
we're going to check

00:13:51.741 --> 00:13:55.620
the distribution of
SNR in a few slides.

00:13:55.621 --> 00:13:59.465
So, we have also noise and
speech level are varying.

00:13:59.466 --> 00:14:01.630
So, all these variables
actually are

00:14:01.631 --> 00:14:05.320
being assessed by people.

00:14:05.321 --> 00:14:09.910
&gt;&gt; I have a question. So,
SNR changes as a huge range,

00:14:09.911 --> 00:14:14.305
but your reviewer doesn't have
a huge range, why is that?

00:14:14.306 --> 00:14:19.220
&gt;&gt; Well those are the
RT60 that we had at hand.

00:14:19.221 --> 00:14:21.340
I think one of the things that we

00:14:21.341 --> 00:14:24.330
discussed is these are
the most common type

00:14:24.331 --> 00:14:27.410
of RT60 that you can find

00:14:27.411 --> 00:14:31.135
in rooms like
such an office, in-house.

00:14:31.136 --> 00:14:33.740
&gt;&gt; So, the higher that this.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:38.325
&gt;&gt; I mean there are some,

00:14:38.326 --> 00:14:42.090
like a treated office
will be 200 or 100,

00:14:42.091 --> 00:14:45.390
and then my living room
is a thousand,

00:14:45.391 --> 00:14:48.290
so why not go from a 100 to 1000?

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:53.730
