00:33:42.386 --> 00:33:47.340
The first approach that we
thought was using I-vector.

00:33:47.341 --> 00:33:49.410
So, the vector has been widely

00:33:49.411 --> 00:33:51.825
used for recognition tasks.

00:33:51.826 --> 00:33:53.580
So that was one of
the motivation.

00:33:53.581 --> 00:33:56.550
So, has been used for a
speaker vilification,

00:33:56.551 --> 00:33:57.935
language recognition,

00:33:57.936 --> 00:34:01.060
emotion recognition
and the question was,

00:34:01.061 --> 00:34:02.310
what about speech quality?

00:34:02.311 --> 00:34:05.830
So you use I-vector for that?

00:34:06.400 --> 00:34:09.250
I-vectors they are known to carry

00:34:09.251 --> 00:34:13.410
speaker and also
channel variability.

00:34:16.600 --> 00:34:19.850
The way you can think
about the I-vector Is,

00:34:19.851 --> 00:34:23.060
you extract several observations

00:34:23.061 --> 00:34:27.025
from your speech file,
usually MFCCs.

00:34:27.026 --> 00:34:29.280
The bottom line is
where want to map

00:34:29.281 --> 00:34:35.370
those features into a
utterance level vector,

00:34:35.371 --> 00:34:37.100
which is the I-vector.

00:34:37.101 --> 00:34:41.245
So we want to map them in
this total variability space.

00:34:41.246 --> 00:34:43.130
So the way we do that is,

00:34:43.131 --> 00:34:45.805
first you compute
the GMM-UBM model.

00:34:45.806 --> 00:34:48.515
So, this background model we have

00:34:48.516 --> 00:34:51.700
bunch of speech files
and it's going

00:34:51.701 --> 00:34:58.240
to be a reference or like
a mean in this space here.

00:34:58.241 --> 00:35:00.810
After computing the GMM-UBM,

00:35:00.811 --> 00:35:03.000
you extract some statistics

00:35:03.001 --> 00:35:07.280
from each one of
your observations here.

00:35:07.281 --> 00:35:09.930
So, you get what is
called the super vector.

00:35:09.931 --> 00:35:12.275
So, the super vector is

00:35:12.276 --> 00:35:17.720
composed by the
components of your GMM,

00:35:17.721 --> 00:35:21.150
and the features that

00:35:21.151 --> 00:35:24.130
you have in your
feature extraction.

00:35:24.131 --> 00:35:26.660
So, these super vector here

00:35:26.661 --> 00:35:32.640
is what you are going to use
to compute the I-vector.

00:35:32.641 --> 00:35:35.445
So m here means
independent super vector,

00:35:35.446 --> 00:35:37.350
which comes from the GMM,

00:35:37.351 --> 00:35:39.570
and you have the
dependent super vector.

00:35:39.571 --> 00:35:41.210
So the dependent super vector

00:35:41.211 --> 00:35:44.020
depends on your speech files.

00:35:44.021 --> 00:35:45.935
Every new speech file,

00:35:45.936 --> 00:35:51.405
you are going to compute
the offset from the GMM-UBM,

00:35:51.406 --> 00:35:55.140
which is mean I-vector.

00:35:55.141 --> 00:35:57.540
To do that you have
also to compute

00:35:57.541 --> 00:36:00.560
the low-rank liability matrix,

00:36:00.561 --> 00:36:01.970
which is the T-matrix.

00:36:01.971 --> 00:36:08.610
We use the UBM and the Baum
Welch statistics to do that.

00:36:08.611 --> 00:36:10.480
So this is the framework for

00:36:10.481 --> 00:36:12.975
the I-vector effort,
preparing this framework,

00:36:12.976 --> 00:36:15.535
we extract the I-vector for

00:36:15.536 --> 00:36:18.410
each utterance in the train set,

00:36:18.411 --> 00:36:21.010
in devset, and in testset.

00:36:21.011 --> 00:36:23.420
So here are some plots of

00:36:23.421 --> 00:36:28.275
the I-vector projecting
the clean speech,

00:36:28.276 --> 00:36:34.605
which is the dark blue dots
here and MOS below 0.5.

00:36:34.606 --> 00:36:37.045
So, you see that it projects

00:36:37.046 --> 00:36:42.360
the I-vector and can actually
separate the two sets.

00:36:42.361 --> 00:36:46.600
So, here's considering MOS
between two and three.

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:49.540
When we consider actually

00:36:49.541 --> 00:36:53.905
the two levels of MOS
with the clean speech,

00:36:53.906 --> 00:36:56.920
we see that the data gets
a little bit mixed up.

00:36:56.921 --> 00:36:59.740
There's some clustering here,

00:36:59.741 --> 00:37:02.980
but there's some limitations.

00:37:02.981 --> 00:37:04.700
So, then we thought maybe you can

00:37:04.701 --> 00:37:07.075
use I-vectors combined with

00:37:07.076 --> 00:37:10.385
a DNN MLP architecture

00:37:10.386 --> 00:37:15.660
to find a better
representation to compute MOS.

00:37:15.661 --> 00:37:19.000
So this is the architecture
that we proposed.

00:37:19.001 --> 00:37:22.170
We have I-vectors
of 400 dimensions.

00:37:22.171 --> 00:37:24.150
I have a two hidden layers,

00:37:24.151 --> 00:37:28.380
200 hidden units first layer
and the second layer used,

00:37:28.381 --> 00:37:31.450
dropout for as
regularization and relu as

00:37:31.451 --> 00:37:34.680
the activation
function and then we

00:37:34.681 --> 00:37:38.900
predict the MOS with this model.

00:37:38.901 --> 00:37:41.550
So, the second approach

00:37:41.551 --> 00:37:46.990
was using what is called a
Q cepstral coefficients.

00:37:46.991 --> 00:37:49.150
Again, it has been

00:37:49.151 --> 00:37:52.620
used in bass cleaning
speaker verification to

00:37:52.621 --> 00:37:57.625
detect natural and
unnatural speech

00:37:57.626 --> 00:37:59.880
and has been very
successful in that.

00:37:59.881 --> 00:38:02.465
So that was one of
the motivations.

00:38:02.466 --> 00:38:06.489
The other one is that, it's
psychoacoustic motivated.

00:38:06.490 --> 00:38:10.015
So, the idea of the constants

00:38:10.016 --> 00:38:14.415
cepstral coefficients
is they say that

00:38:14.416 --> 00:38:17.415
this Q or the quality factor

00:38:17.416 --> 00:38:22.395
increases as the center
frequency increases.

00:38:22.396 --> 00:38:25.765
So as you go up,

00:38:25.766 --> 00:38:28.235
this factor will increase because

00:38:28.236 --> 00:38:30.910
the window length that you

00:38:30.911 --> 00:38:37.030
extract your samples
they are fixed.

00:38:37.031 --> 00:38:39.640
So what they propose is to make

00:38:39.641 --> 00:38:44.600
this Q factor constant
and change this,

00:38:44.601 --> 00:38:46.530
so that this doesn't change.

00:38:46.531 --> 00:38:50.190
So, the motivation for

00:38:50.191 --> 00:38:53.210
that is that we're going
to get a spectrum.

00:38:53.211 --> 00:38:56.700
There is a better resolution

00:38:56.701 --> 00:38:59.270
for high frequencies
and low frequencies.

00:38:59.271 --> 00:39:02.265
So, that's how it
would look like.

00:39:02.266 --> 00:39:09.090
So, you'd have this spectrum
using their model, the CQT.

00:39:09.091 --> 00:39:14.435
So, this is how it's computed.

00:39:14.436 --> 00:39:16.540
So, we compute
the spectrum but we are

00:39:16.541 --> 00:39:18.490
using the Q transform,

00:39:18.491 --> 00:39:20.965
the power spectrum, the log,

00:39:20.966 --> 00:39:23.460
and this is actually
a spectral feature.

00:39:23.461 --> 00:39:29.815
So, they had to change the scale,

00:39:29.816 --> 00:39:32.550
the log scale and
they do that by using

00:39:32.551 --> 00:39:36.840
this uniform and then they
compute the deception.

00:39:38.070 --> 00:39:42.100
We use two approaches
for these features.

00:39:42.101 --> 00:39:43.320
The first one using

00:39:43.321 --> 00:39:49.070
cepstral and the second one
using this spectrum.

00:39:50.440 --> 00:39:52.585
So, if we remove

00:39:52.586 --> 00:39:55.600
the signal process
computation of the spectrum,

00:39:55.601 --> 00:40:00.220
we know that cepstral features
are very well established.

00:40:00.221 --> 00:40:05.415
But if you let the DNN look
at directly on the spectrum,

00:40:05.416 --> 00:40:07.110
would you get better results?

00:40:07.111 --> 00:40:10.290
There was actually
the question here.

00:40:12.870 --> 00:40:16.225
So this is our evaluation.

00:40:16.226 --> 00:40:19.600
We use evolution setup.

00:40:19.601 --> 00:40:21.570
So we use the Pearson
correlation and

00:40:21.571 --> 00:40:24.130
